la .uuz=C3=ACt. cu cusku di'e
> If you change even just one (!) gismu definition, you have to deal with
> these problems:
> - Ambiguity. People may wonder =E2=80=9CHmmm? Did person X mean =E2=80=98=
broda=E2=80=99 in the
> sense BEFORE or AFTER the gismu rewrite?=E2=80=9D.

This is already happening both with gismu and with cmavo. Some people=20
use a word one way, and others use it another way. Try to compare some=20
texts and you will find conflicting usage. Old texts are also full of=20
usage that is now considered incorrect, but reading them and knowing=20
when they were written, we can easily understand why and don't need to=20
be confused. As for gismu, lots of people for instance use {traji}=20
without its old x3 place, lots of people use {mabla} with a new definition.

> - Even if people knew that, lojbanists basically may be split into two
> camps, the =E2=80=9Cbefore gismu rewrite=E2=80=9D and the =E2=80=9Cafter =
gismu rewrite=E2=80=9D camp.

Already the case for cmavo and for several gismu. Consider Lojbab who=20
refuses to use xorlo even though it's official.

> - The existing regular lujvo using this gismu may become irregular. A
> person may wonder =E2=80=9CHuh? Where did that X place come from?=E2=80=
=9D. The lujvo
> would still work, but remembering will be harder.

Regular lujvo will change automatically in usage and where needed the=20
definitions can be updated in the dictionary.

> - This will likely invalidate many texts from before the change, since
> gismu are used so often in the language.

Yes, but it will not make the texts uninterpretable by any means, just=20
like xorlo didn't. There are actually several changes that are part of=20
the BPFK cmavo specification that invalidate large chunks of usage (e.g.=20
ZAhO or VA). They reflect modern usage at the cost of making old texts=20
awkward or simply incorrect. The changes still happened, and it's good=20
that they did, because the new definitions are better. So where is the=20
difference?

I may also add that whatever changes the BPFK or whatever other=20
language-defining body makes to the language, I *will* update my=20
writings to reflect that, and I've written some 60 thousand words. This=20
is not the problem.

> I am not saying these problems are neccessarily unsolvable. But the
> proposal does not seem to address any of these problems.

Well, instead of shooting it down categorically, you could have asked=20
for clarification. Afterall, this is supposed to be a joint effort.

> It would be nice if we just could change the gismu =E2=80=9Cfor free=E2=
=80=9D. But
> actually, the price of changing just one gismu is rather high. And you
> want to change ALL gismu if I understood correctly.

*Looking* at every gismu does not entail *changing* every gismu. All of=20
this is in essence a democratic process, driven by the desires of the=20
community. Nobody is going to change {dunda}, for example. There will be=20
no changes made just for the sake of changing things.

There is a non-negligible number of people who support the revision and=20
who are going to take part. Maybe they are going to speak up themselves.

